0220: realistic flashlight: no beam without the game's flashlight; bounded glow looking into it

Bug (user video, foyer): Luigi walks into the barrier at the web-covered door; for ~5 frames a huge,
flat, hard-edged white wedge filled the left of the picture, "detached from the flashlight". The
barrier shock turns the flashlight off (the game draws no handlight_ef cone mesh) and lights Luigi
with a white, wide spot light (cos edge 0.766, distance falloff k1 1/1900) pointing back at the
camera. Without a cone mesh resolve_flash kept the frame's nearest bright spot light, so the
realistic beam was ray-marched from that light: unshadowed (the game's shadow map is not rendered
from it), its apex hidden behind the pillar, and doubled by the switch-on flare. Vanilla shows no
beam in those frames.

- resolve_flash runs before anything reads g_flash; a frame without the cone mesh has no flashlight
  for the beam (realistic and volumetric) and counts as "off" for the switch-on flare.
- fs_beam: a view ray that looks into the beam (within the cone's half angle of the way back to the
  lens) runs lengthwise through its bright part, so the haze it gathers grows like 1/sin(angle).
  The scattering is scaled by min(sqrt(sin^2(angle) / sin^2(half angle) + 0.36), 1): at most 0.6x
  straight into the lens, 1 for every ray at a larger angle (side and back views are unchanged).
  Luigi walking toward the camera now shows a glow centred on the lens instead of a full-screen
  yellow wash.
- AURORA_BEAM_SHOW=3: heat map of the scattering amount v (red 0..2, green 2..6, blue 8..16).

